241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 89 0 R
/T (Design Consideration:)
/E (Design Consideration:)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (     During the design phase, I kept my engineering requirements in mind as I worked through several robotic-arm problems. These included non-concentric mounting holes, joint alignment, and components that would be difficult to manufacture. I originally hollowed out the links to reduce weight, but after starting the topology studies, I realized I was removing material before fully understanding where I needed it. I went back to a solid design and used topology optimization in ANSYS to identify the main load paths under the estimated maximum loading conditions. The resulting geometry helped me better understand how the loads traveled through each link and where material was needed.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (     Another design consideration was making the robotic arm easier to assemble and maintain. My original idea was to use 25 lb magnets to hold the joints in place and simplify mounting. However, I realized this could become a problem since I was only allowing two magnets per joint. To improve the mounting design, I added four 3.2 mm holes to each joint, so M3 screws could prevent unwanted rotation around the joint's center axis.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (     For the final V1 design, I used ANSYS topology optimization to reduce each link's mass while preserving key mounting features, including screw holes, nut pockets, and bearing inserts. I also adjusted the optimized geometry to make the components easier to manufacture and assemble in future versions.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
