277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (     As I continued developing each subassembly, I used ANSYS to perform structural analysis and topology optimization on the robotic arm's links. I created a custom PETG material profile using Elegoo's material properties and considered how FDM printing would affect the final parts' strength. Since 3D-printed components are anisotropic, I also considered the printing orientation so the main load paths would rely less on interlayer strength.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (For each link, I used a target factor of safety of 2.0 when evaluating the estimated loading conditions. After the initial structural analysis, I used topology optimization to identify areas where I could remove material without significantly affecting the main load paths. I aimed to retain approximately 40% of the original mass while preserving important mounting features.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E (     After each optimization study, I used the resulting geometry as a reference to refine the CAD models and improve their surfaces. I then checked the updated designs against the stress and deflection requirements to evaluate whether the optimized geometry could be used for the final V1 assembly.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
