344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 92 0 R
/T (     Performing kinematic calculations was an important part of understanding how my robotic arm moves. Using MATLAB, I calculated the Jacobian matrix to determine how changes in joint angles affect the end-effector's velocity and position. I then used eigenvalue and eigenvector analysis to identify the principal directions of movement and how effectively the arm could move in each direction.)
/E (     Performing kinematic calculations was an important part of understanding how my robotic arm moves. Using MATLAB, I calculated the Jacobian matrix to determine how changes in joint angles affect the end-effector's velocity and position. I then used eigenvalue and eigenvector analysis to identify the principal directions of movement and how effectively the arm could move in each direction.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 92 0 R
/T (     Using the joint angles [0 45 -70 20 0 -15], I calculated the Jacobian matrix and its eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Figure 1 shows the velocity manipulability ellipsoid, where the longer axes represent directions with better motion capability and the shorter axes represent more limited movement. Figure 2 shows the resulting joint configuration, while Figure 3 displays the calculated Jacobian, eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and principal motion gains.)
/E (     Using the joint angles [0 45 -70 20 0 -15], I calculated the Jacobian matrix and its eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Figure 1 shows the velocity manipulability ellipsoid, where the longer axes represent directions with better motion capability and the shorter axes represent more limited movement. Figure 2 shows the resulting joint configuration, while Figure 3 displays the calculated Jacobian, eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and principal motion gains.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
