474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 100 0 R
/T ( This experiment used two instruments to measure weight: a Bourdon gauge and a load cell. The Bourdon gauge read pressure in PSI, and the reading rose each time a weight was added, reaching 3520 PSI with all the weights loaded. The gauge also had a large zero error. The fitted line reads about 279 PSI at zero input, and the readings on the way down did not retrace the way-up readings. For example, at a 200 PSI input, the gauge read 680 PSI on unloading and 670 PSI on loading. We applied the method of least squares to find the calibration line, correlation coefficient, and standard error. The averaged Bourdon data gave y = 279.1 + 2.0326x, with a correlation coefficient of 0.99995 and a standard error of 12.763 PSI, indicating a very close linear relationship.)
/E ( This experiment used two instruments to measure weight: a Bourdon gauge and a load cell. The Bourdon gauge read pressure in PSI, and the reading rose each time a weight was added, reaching 3520 PSI with all the weights loaded. The gauge also had a large zero error. The fitted line reads about 279 PSI at zero input, and the readings on the way down did not retrace the way-up readings. For example, at a 200 PSI input, the gauge read 680 PSI on unloading and 670 PSI on loading. We applied the method of least squares to find the calibration line, correlation coefficient, and standard error. The averaged Bourdon data gave y = 279.1 + 2.0326x, with a correlation coefficient of 0.99995 and a standard error of 12.763 PSI, indicating a very close linear relationship.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 100 0 R
/T ( The load cell measures weight with strain gauges. When a weight is placed on the platform, it bends, and the strain gauges measure the resulting compression and tension. With all five weights loaded, the load cell read -4.9183 kg. Its last unloading reading was 0.0032 kg, close to zero but not exactly zero. The same least squares method gave y = 0.4963 - 0.9841x for the averaged data, with a correlation coefficient of -0.999999 and a standard error of 0.0029 kg. The line is essentially perfectly linear, and its slope is close to 1 in magnitude.)
/E ( The load cell measures weight with strain gauges. When a weight is placed on the platform, it bends, and the strain gauges measure the resulting compression and tension. With all five weights loaded, the load cell read -4.9183 kg. Its last unloading reading was 0.0032 kg, close to zero but not exactly zero. The same least squares method gave y = 0.4963 - 0.9841x for the averaged data, with a correlation coefficient of -0.999999 and a standard error of 0.0029 kg. The line is essentially perfectly linear, and its slope is close to 1 in magnitude.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 100 0 R
/T (Human error also affected the data, mostly on the Bourdon gauge. Adding the weights quickly gave a different pressure reading than adding them in small steps. The needle usually rested between two marks, so we had to estimate each reading by eye.)
/E (Human error also affected the data, mostly on the Bourdon gauge. Adding the weights quickly gave a different pressure reading than adding them in small steps. The needle usually rested between two marks, so we had to estimate each reading by eye.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [10]
>>
